# Feature request

## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.

Next generates a bunch of files in `/_next/static/` such as 

```
/_next/static/images/something.HASH.jpg
```

Some of these assets are quite minor such as CSS backgrounds, but others may be significant such as company logos. The latter can appear in search engine results such, e.g. google images.

After the developers have re-uploaded an asset, its hash is likely to change. This is a good thing for HTTP caching strategy, but creates an indexing problem as a side-effect. Until search engines have re-indexed the assets, people will be finding images that are now 404. After re-indexing, it won't be necessarily clear for the search engines that the newly found image is a replacement for the old one. That’s because 404 code does not help with establishing this relationship. 

## Describe the solution you'd like

It’d be great if Next.js could handle not found resources in `/_next/static/` in a special way. If someone requests `/_next/static/images/something.WRONG_HASH.jpg`, Next.js can redirect users to the right hash, say, with 302 or 307. Permanent redirects are probably not desired here because they can be cached more aggressively, so re-uploading an old version of an asset can produce a redirect loop. Not sure about this though – I'm not very knowledgeable in how browsers and search engines treat different kinds of redirects.

## Describe alternatives you've considered

For now, I’ve implemented a custom function and it does what I describe. The trick [requires an Express server](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/tree/canary/examples/custom-server-express), which means that my app is no longer something I can deploy to Now.

```ts
const setupRedirectsForChangedStatics = (server: Express) => {
  // /_next/static/images/something-HASH.jpg
  // /_next/static/images/something.WRONG_HASH.jpg
  // /_next/static/images/something.jpg
  // ↓
  // /_next/static/images/something.HASH.jpg
  interface StaticFilePathInfo {
    full: string;
    base: string;
    ending: string;
    hash: string;
    extension: string;
  }

  const parseStaticFilePath = (filePath: string): StaticFilePathInfo => {
    const [ending, hashWithDashOrDot, , , extension] = filePath.match(
      /((-[0-9A-Za-z]{32})|(\.[0-9A-Za-z]{8}))?((\.[A-Za-z]+)+)$/,
    ) || ["", "", "", "", ""];

    const base = ending
      ? filePath.substring(0, filePath.length - ending.length)
      : "";

    return {
      full: filePath,
      base,
      ending,
      hash:
        typeof hashWithDashOrDot === "string"
          ? hashWithDashOrDot.substr(1)
          : "",
      extension,
    };
  };

  const staticFilePaths = globbySync("images/**/*", {
    cwd: resolve(nextDir, "static"),
  });
  const staticFileInfos: StaticFilePathInfo[] = staticFilePaths.map(
    parseStaticFilePath,
  );

  server.get(/^\/_next\/static\/(.*)$/, (req, res, nextHandler) => {
    const relativePath = req.params[0];
    if (staticFilePaths.includes(relativePath)) {
      nextHandler();
      return;
    }
    const { base, extension } = parseStaticFilePath(relativePath);

    const foundFileInfo =
      find(staticFileInfos, { base, extension }) ||
      find(staticFileInfos, { base });
    if (foundFileInfo) {
      return res.redirect(`/_next/static/${foundFileInfo.full}`);
    }
    nextHandler();
  });
};
```

It'd be great if Next.js and Now just took care of redirects from old hashes without me even knowing this. This behaviour seems to fall into a category of ‘reasonable defaults’, unless I’m missing some pitfalls.

